// Package dsp is offline, zero-phase signal processing for Go: IIR filter
// design, filtering as (b, a) or second-order sections, frequency response,
// signal conditioning, peak finding, envelopes, alignment, spectral analysis and
// reductions, checked against SciPy and MATLAB.
//
// # Compatibility
//
// Every function matches its SciPy counterpart within a measured tolerance,
// checked against reference vectors generated by a pinned SciPy release. Each
// figure is the worst on either amd64 or arm64, which round slightly
// differently because arm64 fuses multiply-adds.
//
//	Function         SciPy equivalent                    Worst measured   Tested to
//	Butter           butter (b, a)                       7.6e-15          1e-13
//	Butter + SOS     butter(output='sos')                7.3e-15          1e-13
//	ZPK.SOS          zpk2sos                             1.1e-16          1e-14
//	Filter           lfilter                             6.4e-16          1e-14
//	FilterState      lfilter with zi                     2.3e-16          1e-14
//	FilterZi         lfilter_zi                          3.3e-16          1e-14
//	FiltFilt         filtfilt                            8.8e-16          1e-14
//	SOSFilter        sosfilt, with and without zi        4.1e-14          1e-12
//	SOSFilterZi      sosfilt_zi                          9.8e-17          1e-14
//	SOSFiltFilt      sosfiltfilt                         1.7e-13          1e-11
//	Freqz            freqz                               1.4e-15          1e-13
//	SOSFreqz         freqz_sos                           3.7e-13          1e-11
//	Detrend          detrend                             5.6e-16          1e-14
//	Interp1          interp1d(bounds_error=False)        1.3e-16          1e-14
//	RMS              sqrt(mean(x**2)), from NumPy        1.4e-16          1e-14
//	FindPeaks        find_peaks                          exact            exact
//	PeakProminences  peak_prominences                    exact            1e-14
//	PeakWidths       peak_widths                         exact            1e-14
//	Hilbert          hilbert                             5.7e-16          1e-14
//	Envelope         MATLAB's envelope, from hilbert     1.0e-15          1e-14
//	STFT             stft, unpadded, times sum(window)   4.2e-16          1e-14
//	Spectrogram      spectrogram(mode='psd')             7.3e-16          1e-14
//	Welch            welch                               1.1e-15          1e-13
//	Periodogram      periodogram                         1.1e-15          1e-13
//	Sum              sum, exactly (math.fsum)            exact            1e-14
//	Mean             mean, exactly                       exact            1e-14
//	Diff             numpy.diff                          exact            1e-14
//	Grad             numpy.gradient                      exact            1e-14
//	CumSum           cumsum, exactly                     exact            1e-14
//	Trapz            trapezoid, exactly                  exact            1e-14
//	CumTrapz         cumulative_trapezoid, exactly       exact            1e-14
//	Percentile       numpy.percentile                    exact            1e-14
//
// Errors are relative to the largest expected value, or for filtering to the
// largest input value if that's larger. For scale, 1e-16 is about one unit in
// the last place of a float64. The larger SOS figures all come from one
// deliberately difficult filter (order 8, cutoff 0.01), whose poles sit so close
// to z = 1 that single rounding differences between Go and SciPy are amplified.
// Every other filter agrees within a few units in the last place.
//
// Two very low cutoff filters, order 2 at 0.001 low-pass and 0.0008 high-pass,
// are checked separately. They're the original MATLAB application's own
// designs, and their poles sit even closer to z = 1, so rounding differences
// between implementations are magnified by roughly the square of 1/(1 - pole):
//
//	Function      Worst against SciPy   Tested to   Worst against MATLAB   Tested to
//	Filter        9.5e-13               1e-11       6.2e-13                1e-11
//	FilterState   9.9e-14               1e-12       1.1e-13                1e-11
//	FiltFilt      4.4e-12               1e-10       1.5e-11                1e-9
//	Freqz         1.5e-12               1e-10       1.1e-11                1e-9
//
// SciPy and MATLAB differ from each other on these filters by as much. At DC
// the 0.001 low-pass's gain is exactly 1, and Freqz gives 1 + 1.6e-12 where
// MATLAB gives 1 + 1.3e-11, so much of the difference is MATLAB's.
//
// Functions MATLAB also has are checked against reference vectors generated by
// MATLAB R2025a (Signal Processing Toolbox 25.1), on the same inputs:
//
//	Function                 MATLAB equivalent               Worst measured   Tested to
//	Butter                   [b, a] = butter                 1.2e-13          1e-11
//	Filter                   filter                          6.4e-16          1e-14
//	FilterState              [y, zf] = filter(b, a, x, zi)   3.0e-16          1e-14
//	FiltFilt + MATLABPadLen  filtfilt(b, a, x)               8.8e-16          1e-14
//	SOSFilter                sosfilt                         4.5e-14          1e-12
//	Freqz                    freqz(b, a, n)                  1.9e-15          1e-13
//	SOSFreqz                 freqz(sos, n)                   1.1e-12          1e-10
//	Detrend                  detrend                         1.2e-15          1e-13
//	Interp1                  interp1                         2.2e-16          1e-14
//	FillMissing              fillmissing                     exact            1e-14
//	RMS                      rms                             1.2e-16          1e-14
//	FindPeaks + MATLABPeaks  findpeaks                       exact            exact
//	PeakProminences          [~, ~, ~, p] = findpeaks        exact            1e-14
//	PeakWidths               [~, ~, w] = findpeaks           2.7e-15          1e-13
//	Hilbert                  hilbert                         5.8e-16          1e-14
//	Envelope                 envelope(x), (x, n, "rms")      4.5e-15          1e-13
//	FindSignal               findsignal(data, signal)        5.9e-16          1e-14
//	FindSignal + SearchFast  findsignal(data, signal)        1.5e-15          1e-13
//	STFT                     [S, F, T] = spectrogram         4.2e-16          1e-14
//	STFT + AtFrequencies     spectrogram(x, w, n, f, fs)     2.4e-13          1e-11
//	Spectrogram              [~, F, T, P] = spectrogram      7.3e-16          1e-14
//	Welch                    pwelch                          1.3e-15          1e-13
//	Periodogram              periodogram                     5.0e-16          1e-14
//	Sum                      sum                             9.0e-14          1e-12
//	Mean                     mean                            9.0e-14          1e-12
//	Diff                     diff                            exact            1e-14
//	Grad                     gradient(x, dx)                 2.1e-14          1e-12
//	CumSum                   cumsum                          3.6e-13          1e-11
//	Trapz                    trapz(dx, y)                    9.0e-14          1e-12
//	CumTrapz                 cumtrapz(dx, y)                 3.6e-13          1e-11
//	Percentile + Hazen       prctile                         4.0e-17          1e-14
//
// The largest figures come from the same difficult filter, where MATLAB and
// SciPy differ from each other by as much. Second-order sections aren't
// compared: MATLAB's zp2sos gives a first-order section one zero rather than
// two, and breaks ties in pole radius differently, so it finds the same filter
// in different sections. SOSFiltFilt doesn't yet match MATLAB's
// filtfilt(sos, g, x), which filters section by section. SciPy has no
// fillmissing, so FillMissing is checked against MATLAB's documented definition
// as well as MATLAB itself. MATLAB's RMS envelope is 4.5e-15 from an exactly
// summed answer, where Envelope's compensated sum is within 1e-15 of it, so
// that gap is MATLAB's. SciPy has no findsignal either, so FindSignal is also
// checked against MATLAB's definition summed exactly, which it matches exactly.
// MATLAB's distance is measured relative to the window's and signal's sums of
// squares, the scale it rounds at: its perfect matches are up to 2^-29, not 0.
// STFT with AtFrequencies is within 1 ULP of exact sums, which SciPy has no
// equivalent for. MATLAB's spectrogram at a frequency vector is 2.4e-13 from
// them, even at the FFT's own bins, where its FFT path agrees within 3e-16.
// The sums are compensated, and match exact sums on every SciPy-side case.
// MATLAB sums plainly, so its sums are measured relative to the sum of the
// magnitudes summed, the scale it rounds at: its running sum of 20,000 tenths
// drifts by 3.6e-13 of that, and 1e8 among 20,000 thousandths, then cancelled,
// sums to 20.00001 where Sum gives 20.
//
// SciPy and MATLAB are references for the maths, not specifications. Where
// they differ, dsp usually follows SciPy, with MATLAB's behaviour as an option,
// e.g. MATLABPadLen for filtfilt's padding length. Where neither default suits
// general use, dsp chooses its own and says why, as the spectral functions do.
//
// # Filter representations
//
// Design functions return a ZPK: the filter's zeros, poles and gain. Its BA
// method gives transfer-function coefficients (b, a), for Filter and FiltFilt.
// Its SOS method gives second-order sections, for SOSFilter and SOSFiltFilt.
// (b, a) is simple and matches MATLAB's most common usage, but loses precision
// as the order rises or the band narrows. Second-order sections stay accurate,
// and are the better choice above order 4 or so.
//
// # Frequencies
//
// Band edges and cutoffs are normalised to Nyquist, as in SciPy and MATLAB:
// 0 < wn < 1, where 1 is half the sample rate. For a frequency f in Hz at sample
// rate fs, use f / (fs/2). Freqz and SOSFreqz return frequencies in rad/sample,
// also as SciPy and MATLAB do: divide by pi for Nyquist-normalised.
//
// # Conditioning
//
// Detrend removes a signal's least-squares line, or its mean. Interp1
// interpolates linearly, returning NaN outside the sampled range, as MATLAB's
// interp1 and SciPy's interp1d with bounds_error=False do. (numpy.interp
// differs there: it repeats the end values.) FillMissing replaces NaN samples,
// by linear interpolation across each gap and linear extrapolation at the ends,
// or with a constant, as MATLAB's fillmissing. RMS is the root mean square.
//
// # Peaks and envelopes
//
// FindPeaks finds every local maximum, then narrows them down with
// MinPeakHeight, PeakThreshold, MinPeakProminence, MinPeakWidth and
// MinPeakDistance. PeakProminences and PeakWidths measure given peaks.
//
// FindPeaks follows SciPy's find_peaks by default. MATLAB's findpeaks differs
// in three rules, each its own option, so any combination is available.
// MATLABPeaks sets all three:
//
//	Rule                                    SciPy (default)   MATLAB    Option
//	Sample reported for a flat-topped peak  middle            first     Plateau
//	Which of two close, equal peaks stays   later             earlier   Ties
//	When MinPeakDistance is applied         first             last      DistanceLast
//
// MATLAB's MinPeakDistance d keeps peaks more than d apart, and
// MinPeakDistance keeps them at least d apart, so MATLAB's d is
// MinPeakDistance(d+1).
//
// Hilbert gives the analytic signal. Envelope gives a signal's upper and lower
// envelopes: its mean plus and minus either the analytic signal's magnitude
// (EnvelopeAnalytic) or a moving RMS (EnvelopeRMS), as MATLAB's envelope does.
// SciPy's envelope differs, dropping the Nyquist bin and having no RMS method,
// so Envelope is checked against MATLAB's definition built from SciPy's
// hilbert and NumPy, as well as against MATLAB itself.
//
// # Alignment
//
// FindSignal finds the window of data closest to a signal, by the sum of
// squared differences, with ties to the earliest window, as MATLAB's
// findsignal does with its default options. SignalSearch chooses how every
// window is scored. Measured for a 30,000-sample signal in 400,000 samples:
//
//	SearchMethod   Time    Distance
//	SearchHybrid   29 ms   exact (the default)
//	SearchFast     28 ms   rounded at the scale of the sums of squares, as MATLAB's
//	SearchDirect   77 s    exact
//
// Hybrid scores every window by FFT correlation, then rescores exactly each
// window that could, within the correlation's rounding, be the closest, so it
// gives Direct's answer at close to Fast's cost. On near-periodic data, where
// many windows are almost equally close, it rescores them all and takes about
// twice as long as Fast, whose choice between them is then down to rounding.
//
// # Spectral analysis
//
// STFT transforms a signal segment by segment, and Spectrogram gives each
// segment's power. Welch averages those powers into one spectrum, much less
// noisy than a single transform, and Periodogram transforms the whole signal at
// once. All four take the window as a slice, from tukey/window or anywhere, and
// its length is the segment length. There's no default window or segment
// length: together they set the trade-off between time and frequency
// resolution, and the right choice depends on the signal.
//
// The options share one set of rules:
//
//	Option          Default                                   Applies to
//	Overlap         half the window                           all but Periodogram
//	NFFT            the window's length                       all
//	TwoSided        one-sided, as the signal is real          all
//	SegmentDetrend  none: call Detrend on the whole signal    all
//	Scaling         ScalingDensity, units^2/Hz                all but STFT
//	Average         AverageMean                               Welch
//	AtFrequencies   the FFT's bins                            STFT
//
// STFT is the plain transform of each windowed segment, with its phase measured
// from the segment's start: divide by the window's sum for amplitudes. Each
// segment's time is its centre, (start + len(window)/2)/fs, and a partial last
// segment is dropped. TimeFrequency values are indexed [segment][frequency],
// the transpose of SciPy's and MATLAB's layout. TwoSided orders frequencies as
// fft.Freq does, with the upper half negative, where MATLAB labels it above
// Nyquist.
//
// ScalingDensity is comparable across sample rates and windows: multiplied by
// the frequency spacing and summed, it gives the windowed signal's power.
// ScalingSpectrum, in units^2, reads a sine's power at its peak. One-sided,
// every frequency but DC and Nyquist is doubled to count its negative mirror.
// Welch's median is divided by its bias, so it estimates the same power as the
// mean while ignoring a few segments with transients in them.
//
// AtFrequencies evaluates STFT at any frequencies, to look closely at a band
// without a huge NFFT. Each angle is reduced to a fraction of a turn exactly
// before it's rounded, so the result is within 1 ULP of exact sums.
//
// SciPy and MATLAB choose a window, segment length and overlap for you, and
// differently: SciPy's welch, for instance, uses a 256-sample periodic Hann
// window, half overlapped, and subtracts each segment's mean. Pass the same
// window, Overlap, NFFT and SegmentDetrend to match either.
//
// # Reductions
//
// Sum, Mean, CumSum, Trapz and CumTrapz sum with compensation, so neither a
// long signal nor a few large values among many small ones lose the small
// values' low bits, as a plain sum does. Diff gives the difference between
// each sample and the next, and Grad the slope at each sample: central
// differences inside, one-sided at the ends. Trapz integrates by the
// trapezoidal rule, and CumTrapz gives the integral up to each sample, starting
// at 0, so it lines up with the signal. Any of them given too few samples to
// act on returns an empty slice or 0, never nil, except where there's no
// answer: Mean of an empty signal and Grad of fewer than 2 samples are errors.
//
// Percentile interpolates between the sorted samples, linearly by default, as
// NumPy's default, R's and Excel's PERCENTILE.INC. PercentileBy(PercentileHazen)
// is MATLAB's prctile. Any NaN gives NaN, as a missing sample leaves the
// percentile unknown, where prctile ignores it: FillMissing first to fill gaps.
//
// # Conventions
//
// Every function is offline and stateless: the whole signal goes in as a
// slice, and a new slice comes back. Inputs are never modified, and nothing
// is shared between calls, so all functions are safe for concurrent use.
// To filter in blocks, FilterState and SOSFilterState take a starting state
// and return the final one, to pass to the next block.
//
// Invalid input returns an error wrapping one of the Err sentinels, so check
// for specific failures with errors.Is. Filtering functions don't check that a
// filter is stable. Like SciPy and MATLAB, they assume it is.
package dsp
